WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.214 --> 00:00:03.596
(somber piano music)

00:00:03.596 --> 00:00:06.813
- This is the second worst
day here at IU North.

00:00:09.859 --> 00:00:12.442
(somber music)

00:00:17.155 --> 00:00:22.155
Yesterday, got a Doctor
Bannec, B-A-N-N-E-C,

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:28.137
wanted to send me home.

00:00:28.137 --> 00:00:29.837
"You're not even short of breath."

00:00:31.738 --> 00:00:33.147
I said, "Yes, I am."

00:00:33.147 --> 00:00:35.730
(somber music)

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:40.973
I was in so much pain from my neck.

00:00:40.973 --> 00:00:42.083
My neck hurt so bad.

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:45.533
(somber music)

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:49.513
I was crushed.

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:52.993
It made me feel like I was a drug addict.

00:00:54.376 --> 00:00:56.959
(somber music)

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:01.950
The CT went down a
little bit into my lungs

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:05.240
and you could see new
pulmonary infiltrates,

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:10.240
new lymphadenopathy
all throughout my neck.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:13.457
And all of a sudden, "Yes,
we'll treat your pain."

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:15.720
You have to show proof

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:17.500
that you have something wrong with you.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:19.050
I put forward and I maintained.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:23.728
If I was white, I wouldn't
have to go through that.

00:01:23.728 --> 00:01:26.311
(somber music)

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:32.750
This is how black people get
killed, when you send them home

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:36.370
and they don't know how
to fight for themselves.

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:41.970
I had to talk to somebody,
maybe the media, somebody

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:45.393
to let people know how I'm
being treated up in this place.

00:01:47.728 --> 00:01:50.478
(dramatic music)

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:17.930
- Today we're gonna be
having a very important

00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:21.590
yet difficult conversation
about racism within healthcare,

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:24.100
social disparities within
the healthcare system

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:27.570
and to see what actionable
things we can do to fight back.

00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:30.500
I'd like to send our condolences
to Dr. Moore's family

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:32.470
and a commitment that
I'm gonna make a donation

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:33.870
on their GoFundMe page.

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:35.670
I'm linking that down below as well,

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:37.140
if you'd like to contribute to.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:38.990
In order to have this in-depth discussion,

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:41.710
I've invited special
guests, Dr. Kadijah Ray,

00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:45.090
a pediatric anesthesiologist
and founder of the group,

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:46.570
Physician Women SOAR.

00:02:46.570 --> 00:02:47.650
Let's get right into it.

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:50.130
The difficult part of
having this conversation

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:54.220
after the fact, is that because
of patient privacy rules,

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:56.740
the institution cannot
really adequately say

00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:58.720
what was happening, what the results were.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:01.060
We can only go by the
information that was released

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:02.850
by Dr. Moore herself.

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:06.160
My question to you is based on her videos

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:08.470
and her writings on Facebook,

00:03:08.470 --> 00:03:12.561
how did the topic of racism
enter the conversation

00:03:12.561 --> 00:03:15.320
and how should we be thinking about it

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:18.290
when we're considering her own situation?

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:22.350
- Yeah, I think after a
certain amount of time

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:25.170
of this treatment and this denial

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:29.180
of how serious her symptoms
were as she perceived them,

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:31.977
and as we perceived them, being told,

00:03:31.977 --> 00:03:34.320
"Well, you're really not short of breath."

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:36.830
I mean, anybody watching
that video, you don't have

00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:39.420
to have a medical degree to appreciate

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:41.960
that she could barely
finish her sentences.

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:45.280
One thing that we try to
explain to people in the work

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:49.240
that I do is that we know
what racism looks like.

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:52.839
Just like a woman knows
what sexism looks like.

00:03:52.839 --> 00:03:56.410
There are things and people,
the way people interact,

00:03:56.410 --> 00:03:59.211
certain things that are
said, microaggressions,

00:03:59.211 --> 00:04:03.090
macro aggressions, where,
you know what it looks like.

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:05.270
And that's what she felt was happening.

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:07.460
And she put that name to it.

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:12.356
She felt that it became,
or it was racially driven.

00:04:12.356 --> 00:04:15.300
And maybe the doctor didn't even realize

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:16.440
that was driving him.

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:19.980
And we can talk about that
more later, but there was,

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:22.540
you know, after a point
of being mistreated

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:25.830
and mishandled, she
felt that it was racism.

00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:28.100
- And I've seen your response,

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:30.960
but with the Physician Women's SOAR group

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:34.940
to the response that the organization gave

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:38.500
as to what they thought was
a breakdown in communication.

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:40.220
And one of the main things you pointed out

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:43.060
was that they did not even
address the word racism

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:45.170
in their response.

00:04:45.170 --> 00:04:48.870
Ideally, how would you
have had that conversation

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:51.255
say you were in the position of authority

00:04:51.255 --> 00:04:52.790
overseeing this incident?

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:55.010
How would you have used the word racism?

00:04:55.010 --> 00:04:56.870
What would have been the
ideal approach there?

00:04:56.870 --> 00:04:59.920
- Well, I mean, I understand
that when you're in the shoes

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:02.857
of a big organization, I'm
sure they had their PR people

00:05:02.857 --> 00:05:05.170
and their lawyers and everybody else

00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:08.960
kinda help craft that statement.

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:11.640
But if I was in a position
where I'm making a statement

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:14.520
and I want to have an honest conversation,

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:19.290
you have to be transparent
and you have to not take away

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:20.530
what a person is saying.

00:05:20.530 --> 00:05:23.530
You have to listen to
what the person is saying.

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:27.910
We can't, as institutions,
make pledges to do better,

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:32.430
to do better regarding social
justice, racial justice

00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:35.240
and then not listen when you're being told

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:36.700
that you missed the mark.

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:39.380
And I would just say exactly that.

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:42.770
Dr. Susan Moore did not receive the care

00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:45.400
that she felt she should have received.

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:48.010
She said that it was due to racism,

00:05:48.010 --> 00:05:50.840
and we are going to do everything we can

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:54.470
to investigate this particular situation

00:05:54.470 --> 00:05:57.720
and to educate our team and our staff

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:01.230
so that we can do better by
the community that we serve.

00:06:01.230 --> 00:06:04.000
We all have implicit biases.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:08.130
And unfortunately, sometimes
those implicit biases

00:06:08.130 --> 00:06:12.300
cause issues in the work
that we have been charged

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:13.140
with doing.

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:17.470
- To me, I see huge
inequalities that happen

00:06:17.470 --> 00:06:18.730
with communities of color.

00:06:18.730 --> 00:06:21.570
Access to medications
whether it's financial

00:06:21.570 --> 00:06:24.420
or physical, distance wise.

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:26.870
When I recommend a
healthy diet to a patient,

00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:30.440
do they actually have the
ability to acquire that diet?

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:32.850
Do they live in a food desert?

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:36.210
Like the fact that zip codes
are oftentimes more predictive

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:39.300
of someone's health than
their actual lab values.

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:42.680
All of these things are partial factors

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:45.050
that doctors who think
holistically should be thinking

00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:46.930
about their patients.

00:06:46.930 --> 00:06:51.180
How do we distinguish
systemic inequalities

00:06:51.180 --> 00:06:55.100
that a lot of times were
created because of racism

00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:59.360
in our past, how do we distinguish
between issues like that

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:03.040
and situations like Dr. Moore's

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:07.510
where racism seems to be
a direct sort of claim

00:07:07.510 --> 00:07:09.430
against the people delivering the care?

00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:10.850
How do we balance those two?

00:07:10.850 --> 00:07:15.180
- I think it's really, it's
hard to balance the two.

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:18.140
But we just have to be
honest with ourselves.

00:07:18.140 --> 00:07:20.980
There's a Harvard implicit bias test

00:07:20.980 --> 00:07:22.890
that I think every everybody should take.

00:07:22.890 --> 00:07:24.800
Most people are just shocked (giggles)

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:28.970
at really what it tells
them about themselves.

00:07:28.970 --> 00:07:33.090
And I think that once we
start having a hard look

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:36.530
at ourselves, doing an
active work to undo some

00:07:36.530 --> 00:07:40.120
of these things, then everything
kinda, will work together.

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:44.390
The systemic issues versus
the individual issues.

00:07:44.390 --> 00:07:48.820
But we need to start with
ourselves individually.

00:07:48.820 --> 00:07:53.470
And then the institutions
need to make real commitments

00:07:53.470 --> 00:07:56.090
not just writing a pledge
when something is popular

00:07:56.090 --> 00:07:59.600
in the media, who are you
hiring to do this work?

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:02.130
Who's monitoring this information?

00:08:02.130 --> 00:08:03.260
And where do you start?

00:08:03.260 --> 00:08:06.580
I would almost maintain
that, once you started having

00:08:06.580 --> 00:08:10.300
these conversations with
doctors who are already working,

00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:13.260
who've been in the field
for 20 years, 25 years,

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:15.810
who were at the hospital,
it's a little late.

00:08:15.810 --> 00:08:17.820
I mean, I guess it's never too late,

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:21.480
but we need to start much
earlier in medical school,

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:24.180
having these conversations, these classes,

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:27.220
and really unpacking this information.

00:08:27.220 --> 00:08:29.020
- In a court of law,
you're judging an action.

00:08:29.020 --> 00:08:31.470
Whether or not someone stole
a car or didn't steal a car.

00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:33.130
When you're talking about racism,

00:08:33.130 --> 00:08:35.080
you're talking about partially intent.

00:08:35.080 --> 00:08:38.687
And that requires a lot of difficulty

00:08:38.687 --> 00:08:40.390
'cause unless they outwardly say something

00:08:40.390 --> 00:08:43.200
or write something, it's
almost impossible to prove.

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:46.411
So while that is the case, I
think people take advantage

00:08:46.411 --> 00:08:48.147
of that fact that they say,

00:08:48.147 --> 00:08:50.630
"Oh, you can't prove I was being racist."

00:08:50.630 --> 00:08:53.590
Well, if someone with lived experience

00:08:53.590 --> 00:08:55.660
who understands the microaggressions,

00:08:55.660 --> 00:08:59.500
the tropes as well as you do,
we should trust that person

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:01.380
to be able to make that distinction.

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:03.820
And even if it doesn't mean that it's a,

00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:06.850
a judicial ruling, it
could mean as something

00:09:06.850 --> 00:09:11.000
as trusting an individual to
make the right call, right?

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:12.370
- Correct.

00:09:12.370 --> 00:09:13.203
Correct.
- Yeah.

00:09:13.203 --> 00:09:16.630
- There has to be some
kind of accountability.

00:09:16.630 --> 00:09:18.670
We address that in the Medium article

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:23.430
where the hospital systems
across Indiana made a pledge

00:09:23.430 --> 00:09:25.450
to better serve the communities

00:09:25.450 --> 00:09:29.392
and to start addressing on
racial justice within medicine.

00:09:29.392 --> 00:09:32.420
People have to start holding
each other accountable,

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:35.230
one institution to the
other and, you know,

00:09:35.230 --> 00:09:37.270
help each other out.

00:09:37.270 --> 00:09:39.630
We need to have these conversations

00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:41.420
and really get to the bottom

00:09:41.420 --> 00:09:45.010
of what drives us and
drives our decision-making

00:09:45.010 --> 00:09:48.720
and acknowledge that there's a lot of data

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:49.960
and literature there.

00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:52.660
Open up the New England
Journal of Medicine

00:09:52.660 --> 00:09:57.660
and find so many studies
documented of pain control

00:09:58.000 --> 00:09:59.800
not being the same amongst black.

00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:03.940
Black infants having the
highest mortality in the NICU,

00:10:03.940 --> 00:10:07.480
black women not surviving breast cancer

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:09.670
at the same rate as white women.

00:10:09.670 --> 00:10:12.410
That's not because of
their genetic makeup.

00:10:12.410 --> 00:10:15.030
It's because of later diagnosis,

00:10:15.030 --> 00:10:17.890
not having the same
treatment options offered.

00:10:17.890 --> 00:10:21.160
And by the time these things
are caught, it's too late.

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:23.760
The same with black men
and prostate cancer.

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:25.310
The data is there.

00:10:25.310 --> 00:10:26.710
It's not being made up.

00:10:26.710 --> 00:10:29.710
So if that's there and we know it,

00:10:29.710 --> 00:10:33.320
then we have to start asking
the question of the why,

00:10:33.320 --> 00:10:34.960
how is this happening?

00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:37.840
Why are people of color not receiving

00:10:37.840 --> 00:10:42.453
the same types of physical
exams as their counter parts?

00:10:43.450 --> 00:10:45.370
Why is that happening?

00:10:45.370 --> 00:10:47.560
And then try to do something about it

00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:50.550
instead of then when we get the result

00:10:50.550 --> 00:10:53.677
that goes into this data
and literature saying,

00:10:53.677 --> 00:10:55.800
"Well, but how do we know?"

00:10:55.800 --> 00:10:58.030
I mean, it's there, it's right there.

00:10:58.030 --> 00:10:58.863
- Yeah.

00:10:58.863 --> 00:10:59.696
- And we just have to acknowledge it

00:10:59.696 --> 00:11:01.300
in order to do something about it.

00:11:01.300 --> 00:11:03.870
- Yeah, these disparities existing,

00:11:03.870 --> 00:11:05.887
should not necessarily lead us to say,

00:11:05.887 --> 00:11:08.836
"Well, the whole system is racist."

00:11:08.836 --> 00:11:11.650
At least I don't think it
should be labeled this way.

00:11:11.650 --> 00:11:13.400
You can disagree with me or agree with me.

00:11:13.400 --> 00:11:14.750
I'm curious actually

00:11:14.750 --> 00:11:18.160
but perhaps to say
these disparities exist,

00:11:18.160 --> 00:11:19.950
we need to figure out why.

00:11:19.950 --> 00:11:24.860
And if it's because of past
racist medical research,

00:11:24.860 --> 00:11:29.420
past racist based systemic
inequalities of housing,

00:11:29.420 --> 00:11:34.420
of red lining, that inherently
makes the systemic inequality

00:11:34.810 --> 00:11:36.710
happen as a result of racism,

00:11:36.710 --> 00:11:39.450
not necessarily the fact
that the individuals involved

00:11:39.450 --> 00:11:42.000
in that current system
are racist themselves.

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:45.500
- You have the systemic and
the institutionalized racism

00:11:45.500 --> 00:11:50.500
where you have people
who don't have a quality

00:11:50.530 --> 00:11:54.120
of living, you know, they
don't have the food available.

00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:59.120
And they're living in places
where there's chemical racism

00:11:59.380 --> 00:12:01.100
as far as the environment,

00:12:01.100 --> 00:12:05.040
there's a lot of people doing
some great work in that field.

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:07.100
And then you have the doctor

00:12:07.100 --> 00:12:11.290
who may be from some small town,

00:12:11.290 --> 00:12:13.650
who's never seen a black person,

00:12:13.650 --> 00:12:16.540
and now they're not
doing that physical exam

00:12:16.540 --> 00:12:17.373
the way they should

00:12:17.373 --> 00:12:20.000
and they miss that this
woman has breast cancer.

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:22.138
- I love that with this channel,

00:12:22.138 --> 00:12:24.410
that we can perhaps put some pressure

00:12:24.410 --> 00:12:26.080
on some of these institutions.

00:12:26.080 --> 00:12:29.740
How would you go about encouraging them,

00:12:29.740 --> 00:12:34.200
as a nice word, of making a
change for both black patients,

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:36.180
black communities, minority communities

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:38.980
but also their physicians
and other healthcare workers?

00:12:38.980 --> 00:12:43.530
- We have to put value on lives.

00:12:43.530 --> 00:12:47.350
We can't say that this one
life that's in this suburb

00:12:47.350 --> 00:12:49.920
is more valuable than the life that's here

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:51.115
in this inner city.

00:12:51.115 --> 00:12:54.180
And without resources,
then you're not gonna

00:12:54.180 --> 00:12:56.900
have good doctors who are
gonna wanna go work there.

00:12:56.900 --> 00:12:58.750
Because like you said,
they're gonna get burned out.

00:12:58.750 --> 00:13:00.820
They're not gonna last,
they're gonna leave.

00:13:00.820 --> 00:13:05.820
But until these institutions
make a real commitment and say,

00:13:08.117 --> 00:13:12.110
"These communities are just
as valuable as the next.

00:13:12.110 --> 00:13:16.200
And we have to find
ways to put the money in

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:19.040
so that we have the
resources so that our doctors

00:13:19.040 --> 00:13:21.160
and our staff are supported

00:13:21.160 --> 00:13:23.520
so they can do the jobs
that they need to do,"

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:27.500
it's a really tough hill to climb.

00:13:27.500 --> 00:13:31.070
And then once you get past that,

00:13:31.070 --> 00:13:33.610
then what are we going to do internally

00:13:33.610 --> 00:13:36.880
to assure that we have the skills?

00:13:36.880 --> 00:13:39.040
And I don't mean just the medical skills,

00:13:39.040 --> 00:13:43.458
but all the skills that we
need to be able to communicate,

00:13:43.458 --> 00:13:47.700
educate, interact, and serve the community

00:13:47.700 --> 00:13:49.320
in which we're choosing to be in

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:51.430
in whatever institution that is.

00:13:51.430 --> 00:13:53.290
- You've mentioned also
some great practical points

00:13:53.290 --> 00:13:56.670
for providers, as far as
knowing your obligation,

00:13:56.670 --> 00:14:00.150
being introspective, going
past the science and thinking

00:14:00.150 --> 00:14:02.917
about lived experiences
and communication factors.

00:14:02.917 --> 00:14:07.080
Anything, if anything, do
you think that you would say

00:14:07.080 --> 00:14:12.080
to patients, perhaps minority
patients who feel like,

00:14:13.020 --> 00:14:15.890
that they would like to do
something to improve the quality

00:14:15.890 --> 00:14:16.723
of their care?

00:14:16.723 --> 00:14:18.920
Is there anything you
counsel your patients on?

00:14:18.920 --> 00:14:20.710
- Well, you know, it's interesting

00:14:20.710 --> 00:14:23.190
because this question it's come up a lot.

00:14:23.190 --> 00:14:28.190
I am, I did a radio show
for a show in Minnesota

00:14:28.200 --> 00:14:31.100
where people were asking
like, "Wait a minute,

00:14:31.100 --> 00:14:34.590
if this doctor couldn't
advocate for herself,

00:14:34.590 --> 00:14:36.153
then how are we supposed to

00:14:36.153 --> 00:14:38.070
when we don't speak the language,

00:14:38.070 --> 00:14:40.675
we don't have the
knowledge or the resources?

00:14:40.675 --> 00:14:44.970
But what I can, what I
try to tell people is to

00:14:44.970 --> 00:14:48.840
do your best to advocate for yourself.

00:14:48.840 --> 00:14:51.040
If a doctor is speaking to you

00:14:51.040 --> 00:14:55.010
and you don't understand
what they're saying,

00:14:55.010 --> 00:14:57.710
you know, what tests they're ordering,

00:14:57.710 --> 00:15:00.581
why they're telling you, you
need to take a medication,

00:15:00.581 --> 00:15:05.581
ask them to explain it
again until you understand.

00:15:06.019 --> 00:15:10.480
If you feel like you are being mistreated,

00:15:10.480 --> 00:15:14.500
then if it's an option
to seek another opinion

00:15:14.500 --> 00:15:17.412
or to switch doctors, then do so.

00:15:17.412 --> 00:15:21.270
If you feel like you're
not getting the care

00:15:21.270 --> 00:15:23.510
and the treatment that you deserve,

00:15:23.510 --> 00:15:27.530
then know that you can
get a patient advocate

00:15:27.530 --> 00:15:30.840
within the hospital to
come and be by your side,

00:15:30.840 --> 00:15:34.003
to try to help you navigate the system.

00:15:35.150 --> 00:15:38.780
And then I would tell people, if you feel

00:15:38.780 --> 00:15:41.520
like you're not getting what you need,

00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:43.750
then maybe try to find a doctor

00:15:43.750 --> 00:15:46.110
that you can better relate to.

00:15:46.110 --> 00:15:49.788
If you are a man and you
feel more comfortable

00:15:49.788 --> 00:15:53.630
with a male physician,
then maybe seek that out

00:15:53.630 --> 00:15:56.510
so that you're able to better communicate

00:15:56.510 --> 00:15:59.740
and openly discuss your medical concerns

00:16:00.780 --> 00:16:03.280
so that you can get the
care that you deserve.

00:16:03.280 --> 00:16:05.990
- And this isn't an
obligation for patients.

00:16:05.990 --> 00:16:07.243
It's our obligation to make sure

00:16:07.243 --> 00:16:09.320
that the patient understands.

00:16:09.320 --> 00:16:11.183
When you're listening
to a patient's reaction

00:16:11.183 --> 00:16:14.470
to your instructions, is
the same when a doctor

00:16:14.470 --> 00:16:17.390
is performing a speculum exam
should be making eye contact

00:16:17.390 --> 00:16:19.840
with the patient as well to
make sure they're comfortable.

00:16:19.840 --> 00:16:22.100
So it's the doctor's obligation

00:16:22.100 --> 00:16:23.790
and the institution's obligation

00:16:23.790 --> 00:16:25.930
to make sure the patient's
getting good care.

00:16:25.930 --> 00:16:27.880
The reason we're giving these extra bits

00:16:27.880 --> 00:16:30.310
or you're giving these tidbits to patients

00:16:30.310 --> 00:16:32.000
is to go the extra mile.

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:33.880
And it's not their
responsibility to do this.

00:16:33.880 --> 00:16:36.180
We have to constantly
remind providers that

00:16:36.180 --> 00:16:37.013
because they say,

00:16:37.013 --> 00:16:39.330
"Well, then why didn't the
patient advocate for themselves?"

00:16:39.330 --> 00:16:43.220
Well, while they can,
and that oftentimes fails

00:16:43.220 --> 00:16:44.998
as seen in Dr. Moore's case.

00:16:44.998 --> 00:16:49.100
It's just something that I
would tell my family member

00:16:49.100 --> 00:16:53.160
almost to expect the medical
system at times to fail.

00:16:53.160 --> 00:16:56.540
And if they're able to advocate
and educate themselves,

00:16:56.540 --> 00:16:59.270
they will statistically
have better outcomes.

00:16:59.270 --> 00:17:02.640
And perhaps that will not
solve all our problems

00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:06.510
but it can at least give
some practical advice

00:17:06.510 --> 00:17:09.420
for those who are
interested in seeking it.

00:17:09.420 --> 00:17:14.420
How do we go about talking
of, about racism historically

00:17:15.360 --> 00:17:18.490
and present, without further deteriorating

00:17:18.490 --> 00:17:20.380
the doctor patient
relationship that exists

00:17:20.380 --> 00:17:23.640
between the black community,
as an example, and doctors?

00:17:23.640 --> 00:17:26.390
- We can't put that acknowledgement

00:17:26.390 --> 00:17:29.891
potentially causing a
problem back on the community

00:17:29.891 --> 00:17:31.980
that has been wronged.

00:17:31.980 --> 00:17:32.837
So the-
- For sure.

00:17:32.837 --> 00:17:35.827
- The talking about it
and the conversations,

00:17:35.827 --> 00:17:37.890
the acknowledgement is not the issue

00:17:37.890 --> 00:17:41.430
or the problem of the
black community, okay?

00:17:41.430 --> 00:17:44.580
So we have to, 'cause we know.

00:17:44.580 --> 00:17:47.650
There's a wonderful book
called "Medical Apartheid"

00:17:47.650 --> 00:17:50.800
that talks about how these,

00:17:50.800 --> 00:17:55.600
while the medical establishment
has not documented

00:17:55.600 --> 00:17:59.450
a lot of the abuses, these situations,

00:17:59.450 --> 00:18:02.020
these experiences have been passed down

00:18:02.020 --> 00:18:07.020
amongst our communities even
verbally for hundreds of years.

00:18:08.370 --> 00:18:10.450
So it's well known.

00:18:10.450 --> 00:18:12.880
So what do we do, for example,

00:18:12.880 --> 00:18:16.680
when we are now faced
with the COVID vaccine

00:18:16.680 --> 00:18:20.130
and how do we get communities
to then say, "Okay, well,

00:18:20.130 --> 00:18:22.600
I'm gonna go get this vaccine,"

00:18:22.600 --> 00:18:25.870
when there's this long
historical relationship

00:18:25.870 --> 00:18:27.910
where we haven't been treated that well,

00:18:27.910 --> 00:18:29.713
we've been tricked many times.

00:18:31.310 --> 00:18:36.210
It comes into education, having
people who look like them,

00:18:36.210 --> 00:18:39.890
who they trust, providing that information

00:18:39.890 --> 00:18:43.350
and being available to answer questions

00:18:43.350 --> 00:18:45.610
which is a whole other conversation

00:18:45.610 --> 00:18:50.070
about how we are
underrepresented in medicine

00:18:50.070 --> 00:18:52.100
and in medical school and why?

00:18:52.100 --> 00:18:54.130
That's a whole other conversation.

00:18:54.130 --> 00:18:57.110
But there are plenty of
studies that have been done

00:18:57.110 --> 00:19:00.400
that show that black patients do better

00:19:00.400 --> 00:19:02.360
with doctors who look like them.

00:19:02.360 --> 00:19:04.100
Instead of saying that

00:19:04.100 --> 00:19:08.760
those communities don't trust
the medical establishment,

00:19:08.760 --> 00:19:12.160
we have to change our
speech, which then starts

00:19:12.160 --> 00:19:14.010
to change the way we think.

00:19:14.010 --> 00:19:18.400
The medical establishment has
not done what they need to do

00:19:18.400 --> 00:19:21.723
to earn the trust of those communities.

00:19:22.780 --> 00:19:25.940
- So, if I'm hearing you
right, just to reiterate,

00:19:25.940 --> 00:19:27.780
in order for us to do better

00:19:27.780 --> 00:19:29.890
in the medical space
to treat our patients,

00:19:29.890 --> 00:19:34.890
because ultimately as a whole,
we wanna help everybody.

00:19:34.940 --> 00:19:37.600
That's the goal, the oath
that we take as physicians,

00:19:37.600 --> 00:19:40.470
as nurses, we need to do better

00:19:40.470 --> 00:19:42.680
in reaching out to communities,

00:19:42.680 --> 00:19:46.100
creating more diversity
within our own groups

00:19:46.100 --> 00:19:50.600
so that they can communicate
better and take this long road

00:19:50.600 --> 00:19:54.760
to improving our ability to communicate

00:19:54.760 --> 00:19:57.250
with the black community for example.

00:19:57.250 --> 00:19:58.620
- Yes, that's correct.

00:19:58.620 --> 00:20:02.620
For example, you know, you
have primary care doctors

00:20:02.620 --> 00:20:05.550
who say, "Oh my goodness,
it's just so frustrating.

00:20:05.550 --> 00:20:09.100
Mrs. so-and-so just won't take
the blood pressure medicine,"

00:20:09.100 --> 00:20:12.427
or, "won't take their diabetic
medications," or, you know,

00:20:12.427 --> 00:20:15.090
"they're just not
compliant, just don't..."

00:20:15.090 --> 00:20:20.090
You have to have a conversation,
a real human conversation,

00:20:20.800 --> 00:20:23.830
and this can apply to any patient really.

00:20:23.830 --> 00:20:25.790
The why, why is that?

00:20:25.790 --> 00:20:27.470
Is it access?

00:20:27.470 --> 00:20:29.410
Is it financial?

00:20:29.410 --> 00:20:31.470
Is it some kind of belief system

00:20:31.470 --> 00:20:34.890
where they're afraid
to take the medication?

00:20:34.890 --> 00:20:38.280
If you really have an honest
conversation with yourself

00:20:38.280 --> 00:20:42.380
as a physician, did you
stop and really explain

00:20:42.380 --> 00:20:45.960
and assured that they
understood the importance

00:20:45.960 --> 00:20:49.120
of whatever it is, whatever
treatment, tests, whatever it is

00:20:49.120 --> 00:20:50.720
that you're trying to communicate?

00:20:50.720 --> 00:20:53.080
Did you communicate in a
way that they understand?

00:20:53.080 --> 00:20:56.417
Did you read the body language to see

00:20:56.417 --> 00:20:58.670
was there some kind of
trepidation on the part

00:20:58.670 --> 00:21:02.170
of the patient and really
get to the bottom of why?

00:21:02.170 --> 00:21:05.748
- A good friend and colleague
of mine, Dr. Judy Washington

00:21:05.748 --> 00:21:09.060
actually works with the STFM organization

00:21:09.060 --> 00:21:11.300
to increase diversity in medicine,

00:21:11.300 --> 00:21:12.980
to have this conversation.

00:21:12.980 --> 00:21:15.570
They actually have an
underrepresented minority fund

00:21:15.570 --> 00:21:16.900
to help with scholarships.

00:21:16.900 --> 00:21:18.960
Is this the sole solution

00:21:18.960 --> 00:21:23.170
or do we need to think past
scholarships, past admissions?

00:21:23.170 --> 00:21:24.740
Is there something we're missing here

00:21:24.740 --> 00:21:26.040
that we haven't yet tried?

00:21:26.040 --> 00:21:29.927
- Oh boy, Dr. Mike, that's
a whole other segment.

00:21:29.927 --> 00:21:31.920
(Dr. Kadijah laughing)

00:21:31.920 --> 00:21:34.980
Yeah, I mean, it does go
past the scholarships.

00:21:34.980 --> 00:21:38.150
By that time, it's almost too late.

00:21:38.150 --> 00:21:40.640
We have to start by figuring out

00:21:40.640 --> 00:21:43.010
what's going on in the
public school system?

00:21:43.010 --> 00:21:45.160
What's going on in the inner cities?

00:21:45.160 --> 00:21:47.570
What's happening with the STEM programs

00:21:47.570 --> 00:21:49.750
in those schools and those children,

00:21:49.750 --> 00:21:52.970
when they're in elementary school?

00:21:52.970 --> 00:21:54.697
They don't have the resources.

00:21:54.697 --> 00:21:58.800
And I think COVID has exposed
a lot of those disparities.

00:21:58.800 --> 00:22:00.610
People don't even have computers

00:22:00.610 --> 00:22:03.540
or access to wifi and the internet.

00:22:03.540 --> 00:22:06.620
So how could they possibly, you know,

00:22:06.620 --> 00:22:09.020
get involved in and do
what they need to do

00:22:09.020 --> 00:22:12.110
for some of these classes and programs?

00:22:12.110 --> 00:22:17.110
You know, there are issues
at the primary school level,

00:22:17.380 --> 00:22:21.300
the inner city schools and we
are missing potential doctors

00:22:21.300 --> 00:22:23.390
because they're just not being fostered.

00:22:23.390 --> 00:22:24.800
They're going to high schools

00:22:24.800 --> 00:22:27.330
that don't have a strong AP program.

00:22:27.330 --> 00:22:30.670
If you don't have AP
classes or honors classes

00:22:30.670 --> 00:22:33.580
then how are you gonna get
into a competitive college?

00:22:33.580 --> 00:22:37.740
So it goes back to that
institutionalized racism

00:22:37.740 --> 00:22:42.390
that nobody likes to discuss,
but it's certainly there

00:22:42.390 --> 00:22:46.040
where there are children
all over this country

00:22:46.040 --> 00:22:50.020
who don't have the same
opportunities and while their minds

00:22:50.020 --> 00:22:53.860
and their capabilities
are just like others,

00:22:53.860 --> 00:22:56.030
there's nothing there to foster it.

00:22:56.030 --> 00:22:57.950
They don't have the programming.

00:22:57.950 --> 00:23:00.850
And so they don't have those
opportunities to even go

00:23:00.850 --> 00:23:04.230
to a college that would
be competitive enough

00:23:04.230 --> 00:23:05.290
to prepare them.

00:23:05.290 --> 00:23:09.020
I grew up in the inner
city, South side of Chicago.

00:23:09.020 --> 00:23:11.563
And there is a program that's been around

00:23:11.563 --> 00:23:13.430
for a very long time

00:23:13.430 --> 00:23:15.610
since the seventies called the CAHMCP,

00:23:15.610 --> 00:23:18.710
Chicago Area Health and
Medical Careers Program.

00:23:18.710 --> 00:23:21.487
And it was a housed over at the IIT.

00:23:22.580 --> 00:23:25.530
There was a college, IIT on
the South side of Chicago

00:23:25.530 --> 00:23:28.520
and that was what their
program was all about.

00:23:28.520 --> 00:23:32.810
Taking young children who
had some kind of propensity

00:23:32.810 --> 00:23:36.560
or interest in science and
math and following them

00:23:36.560 --> 00:23:38.650
all the way through
high school, all the way

00:23:38.650 --> 00:23:42.080
to medical school and
without a program like that,

00:23:42.080 --> 00:23:43.540
coming from the inner city,

00:23:43.540 --> 00:23:45.860
I don't think I would be
sitting here with you today.

00:23:45.860 --> 00:23:48.540
- That's such a sad point to hear,

00:23:48.540 --> 00:23:50.390
but an important point to hear

00:23:50.390 --> 00:23:53.710
that young folks are trapped
in this vicious cycle

00:23:53.710 --> 00:23:56.750
that either they're not exposed
to good education early on

00:23:56.750 --> 00:23:59.090
or even if they have interests early on,

00:23:59.090 --> 00:24:00.750
their communities are not set up

00:24:00.750 --> 00:24:03.577
to foster that sort of enthusiasm

00:24:03.577 --> 00:24:07.960
or they get pulled into
crime ridden communities.

00:24:07.960 --> 00:24:11.420
And as a result, they lose
focus on their education

00:24:11.420 --> 00:24:15.120
or it's not a knock at all
on STFM and the scholarships

00:24:15.120 --> 00:24:16.770
and the amazing work that they're doing.

00:24:16.770 --> 00:24:17.990
It's just showing that,

00:24:17.990 --> 00:24:20.060
while that is part of what we need to do,

00:24:20.060 --> 00:24:22.643
there's this whole other
under-addressed section

00:24:22.643 --> 00:24:24.510
of the beginning,

00:24:24.510 --> 00:24:28.590
of how we get students to
get these scholarships.

00:24:28.590 --> 00:24:30.580
And the reality of the fact,

00:24:30.580 --> 00:24:33.020
when we look at a lot of
the assistance programs

00:24:33.020 --> 00:24:36.230
that we have, even in my
hospital where I work,

00:24:36.230 --> 00:24:38.870
a lot of them go unclaimed.

00:24:38.870 --> 00:24:42.332
And it's partially because
our lack of communication,

00:24:42.332 --> 00:24:45.309
it's because failed communication.

00:24:45.309 --> 00:24:48.550
And because we're not
addressing that initial stage

00:24:48.550 --> 00:24:51.670
that you talked about,
of starting people up

00:24:51.670 --> 00:24:55.040
to know that these programs
exist, to get them excited

00:24:55.040 --> 00:24:58.940
about becoming a pediatric
anesthesiologists like yourself.

00:24:58.940 --> 00:25:02.900
And that initial start is
such an important place

00:25:02.900 --> 00:25:04.920
to talk about and focus on

00:25:04.920 --> 00:25:07.740
that I really hope as we move forward

00:25:07.740 --> 00:25:11.980
in the era of investing into
healthcare, into children,

00:25:11.980 --> 00:25:15.330
we really think about
that educational aspect.

00:25:15.330 --> 00:25:17.640
And I hope that's where
a lot of change happens.

00:25:17.640 --> 00:25:20.730
I'm one of those perhaps naive believers

00:25:20.730 --> 00:25:24.220
that if with education, you
could change almost everything.

00:25:24.220 --> 00:25:26.150
If you have an educated patient,

00:25:26.150 --> 00:25:28.000
in that, it won't solve every problem,

00:25:28.000 --> 00:25:29.730
it'll improve their outcomes.

00:25:29.730 --> 00:25:31.470
If you have education to young folks

00:25:31.470 --> 00:25:33.720
they'll be excited to be providers

00:25:33.720 --> 00:25:35.710
and then be providers for the people

00:25:35.710 --> 00:25:39.470
who perhaps look more like
them and have better outcomes.

00:25:39.470 --> 00:25:44.280
So I really would like to see
more incentivization early on

00:25:44.280 --> 00:25:47.007
as opposed to going to high
school solely and saying,

00:25:47.007 --> 00:25:49.540
"Well, if you go, we can
give you this scholarship,"

00:25:49.540 --> 00:25:51.090
that early on aspect is something

00:25:51.090 --> 00:25:53.060
I'm really passionate about as well.

00:25:53.060 --> 00:25:54.890
- Yeah, I think, I
mean, I think it's great

00:25:54.890 --> 00:25:56.630
those scholarships and the scholarships

00:25:56.630 --> 00:25:58.810
like the one that your friend is doing

00:25:58.810 --> 00:26:01.770
is important and again,
all things like that

00:26:01.770 --> 00:26:05.440
is what allowed me to
get through the system

00:26:05.440 --> 00:26:07.230
and many of my friends.

00:26:07.230 --> 00:26:09.320
So it's all very important.

00:26:09.320 --> 00:26:11.470
We just have to continue to look

00:26:11.470 --> 00:26:15.020
at every level because we're missing out

00:26:15.020 --> 00:26:19.970
on a lot of people who could do very well

00:26:19.970 --> 00:26:22.380
but they're just not given the opportunity

00:26:22.380 --> 00:26:24.280
and they don't have that access.

00:26:24.280 --> 00:26:26.070
- I know we covered a lot.

00:26:26.070 --> 00:26:28.301
I'd love to leave you with the last word.

00:26:28.301 --> 00:26:31.210
What do you think should
be the ultimate message

00:26:31.210 --> 00:26:32.510
to the million plus people

00:26:32.510 --> 00:26:34.407
hopefully that will watch this video?

00:26:34.407 --> 00:26:37.910
- I think that I would just
like to impart on people

00:26:37.910 --> 00:26:40.520
that the discussion about race

00:26:40.520 --> 00:26:43.460
is a very difficult discussion

00:26:43.460 --> 00:26:46.410
but it's one that's necessary.

00:26:46.410 --> 00:26:51.380
People are dying and people
are dying needlessly so

00:26:51.380 --> 00:26:55.270
because of, just because
of the color of their skin

00:26:55.270 --> 00:26:57.320
and that's unacceptable.

00:26:57.320 --> 00:26:59.680
We have to reach out to our neighbors

00:26:59.680 --> 00:27:03.710
and try to understand each
other, have conversations,

00:27:03.710 --> 00:27:05.420
get to know each other

00:27:05.420 --> 00:27:09.700
and don't deny other people's experiences.

00:27:09.700 --> 00:27:14.170
My condolences go out to
Dr. Susan Moore's family.

00:27:14.170 --> 00:27:18.720
We lost a big part of
our medical community.

00:27:18.720 --> 00:27:22.850
And I think that we should
all walk away from that.

00:27:22.850 --> 00:27:25.610
Just remembering that when somebody,

00:27:25.610 --> 00:27:28.370
when a black person or a person of color

00:27:28.370 --> 00:27:31.390
or whatever the situation
is, when somebody speaks

00:27:31.390 --> 00:27:35.050
and they're saying
something, we have to listen.

00:27:35.050 --> 00:27:38.040
She knows what, she knew what racism was.

00:27:38.040 --> 00:27:43.040
She called it, and it may have
possibly cost her her life.

00:27:45.690 --> 00:27:50.690
I say that Dr. Moore passed
away due to two things,

00:27:51.070 --> 00:27:55.060
two pandemics, the
pandemic, the COVID pandemic

00:27:55.060 --> 00:27:56.940
and the racism pandemic.

00:27:56.940 --> 00:28:00.030
We have to have these conversations

00:28:00.030 --> 00:28:02.861
so that we can take that off of the table.

00:28:02.861 --> 00:28:05.630
People need to be treated fairly.

00:28:05.630 --> 00:28:08.600
We know what our experiences are

00:28:08.600 --> 00:28:11.480
and just because you
have not experienced it,

00:28:11.480 --> 00:28:14.220
you have to approach these conversations

00:28:14.220 --> 00:28:16.140
with an open heart and ear

00:28:16.140 --> 00:28:19.440
and have some empathy so that
we can get some real change.

00:28:19.440 --> 00:28:21.130
- Thank you so much, Dr. Ray.

00:28:21.130 --> 00:28:23.500
I'm linking all of your
information down below

00:28:23.500 --> 00:28:27.220
where people can find you,
your Physician SOAR group

00:28:27.220 --> 00:28:30.680
and to continue their education
much like I need to do

00:28:30.680 --> 00:28:34.000
in reading "Medical
Apartheid", other resources

00:28:34.000 --> 00:28:36.160
to continue learning,
'cause we're all imperfect.

00:28:36.160 --> 00:28:37.720
We all have implicit biases.

00:28:37.720 --> 00:28:39.530
And I thank everybody for watching

00:28:39.530 --> 00:28:42.540
and Dr. Ray for her wise words and advice.

00:28:42.540 --> 00:28:45.123
(upbeat music)

